<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Gawan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1411–1481</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Khwaja Mahmud Gawan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Gawan was a Persian merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> who became chief minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, serving from the 1460s until 1481. He reorganised the provinces and revenue system, led campaigns that extended Bahmani territory to the west coast, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capture-of-goa">capture of Goa</a>, and founded a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>. Accused of treason through a forged letter, he was executed on the orders of Muhammad Shah III in 1481.</p></div>
